(** The documentation of the keywords, which have no source: cesia doc reads it, and cesia test runs its examples. Each one has
    a block whose first line is the word. The first twenty are reserved, and no name can be one; the last four mean something
    in some places only. Nothing here is code but the uses. *)
use std/set
use std/queue as line

(** if
    A choice, and a value: if a condition, then the value of its branch, else that of the other one. Without else, a false
    condition gives missing. else if chains choices under one end, and a branch can hold many lines.

      if 2 > 1 "yes" else "no" end --> "yes"
      if false 1 end --> missing
      if 0 > 1 "up" else if 0 < 1 "down" else "flat" end --> "down" *)

(** else
    The branch of an if taken when the condition is false, and the arm of a match taken when no pattern fits.

      if false "a" else "b" end --> "b"
      match 7 1 -> "one" else -> "other" end --> "other" *)

(** end
    Closes a block: if, for, while, match, a defn, and the block forms of struct and interface. One end closes an if with all
    its else if.

      if true 1 end --> 1 *)

(** for
    Runs its body once for each element of an array or a range, in order. In brackets, it builds a list: the comprehension,
    which an if after it filters. A dictionary is no array: loop over Keys(d) or Pairs(d).

      total = 0
      for x in [1, 2, 3] total += x end
      total --> 6
      [x * x for x in 1..4] --> [1, 4, 9, 16]
      [x for x in 1..6 if IsEven(x)] --> [2, 4, 6] *)

(** in
    After for, what the loop runs over. Elsewhere, a question: is the value an element of an array, or a key of a dictionary?

      2 in [1, 2, 3] --> true
      "a" in {"a": 1} --> true
      [k for k in Keys({"a": 1, "b": 2})] --> ["a", "b"] *)

(** while
    Runs its body again and again, as long as the condition is true.

      count = 1
      while count < 100 count *= 2 end
      count --> 128 *)

(** break
    Leaves the loop it is in at once, for or while, and goes on after its end.

      seen = 0
      for x in 1..10 if x > 3 break end seen += 1 end
      seen --> 3 *)

(** return
    Leaves the function at once, with a value. A return alone gives missing. Without one, a function gives the value of its last
    line.

      defn sign(x) if x < 0 return "negative" end "not negative" end
      sign(-2) --> "negative"
      sign(5) --> "not negative" *)

(** defn
    Defines a function whose body is a block, up to end. A one-line function needs no defn: Twice(x) = 2 * x. A method writes its
    object first, defn (r Rect) Area(), and a static method its struct before its name, defn Rect.Square(side). A capital first
    letter makes it public, and a public one needs a comment.

      defn twice(x) x * 2 end
      twice(4) --> 8
      thrice(x) = x * 3
      thrice(4) --> 12 *)

(** match
    Compares a value with patterns, in order, and gives the value of the first arm that fits. A pattern is a value, a name that
    takes the value, _ that takes anything, or a shape: [a, b], {Kind: "box", Width: w}, Ok(v), Point(x, y). A name in a pattern
    always takes a new value: it never compares with a variable. An if after a pattern adds a condition, and else takes what no
    arm took; with no else, a value that fits nothing stops the program.

      match 3 1 -> "one" else -> "many" end --> "many"
      match [1, 2] [a, b] -> a + b else -> 0 end --> 3
      match {Kind: "box", Width: 4} {Kind: "box", Width: w} -> w else -> 0 end --> 4
      match -5 n if n < 0 -> "negative" _ -> "other" end --> "negative" *)

(** struct
    Declares a value with named fields, on one line or as a block with one field per line up to end. A field may have a
    default and a type. Every struct has From, which builds one from a dictionary, and a value never changes in place: a
    method gives a new one.

      struct Cup { Size, Full = false }
      Cup(2).Full --> false
      Cup(2, true).Full --> true
      Cup(Size = 3).Size --> 3 *)

(** interface
    Lists methods with no code: a value meets it when it has each of them with that number of parameters, written apart from
    the object. Nothing declares it, as in Go. An interface is a type, and a method on it is a method of every value that meets
    it.

      interface Holder { Volume() }
      (c Cup) Volume() = c.Size * 10
      measure(h Holder) = h.Volume()
      measure(Cup(5)) --> 50 *)

(** and
    True if both sides are true. The right side runs only when the left one is true. Both are true or false: missing is no
    condition.

      true and false --> false
      false and Panic("never runs") --> false *)

(** or
    True if either side is true. The right side runs only when the left one is false.

      false or true --> true
      true or Panic("never runs") --> true *)

(** not
    The opposite of a condition, which is true or false.

      not (1 > 2) --> true *)

(** then
    Passes the value on its left to the expression on its right, which reads it as it: a pipeline read from left to right.

      [1, 2, 3] then Map(it * it, it) then Sum(it) --> 14
      5 then it + 1 --> 6 *)

(** use
    Makes a package or a module visible in a file, by the last part of its path: use std/set gives set. The path says where it
    comes from: std/ is what comes with Cesia, its libraries and the modules of the binary (std/rope, std/sys); deps/ is another
    project; any other path is a folder of this project, from its root (src/shapes). The files of one package need none to see
    each other.

      set.Count(set.Of([1, 1, 2])) --> 2 *)

(** true
    One of the two values of a condition.

      1 < 2 --> true *)

(** false
    The other value of a condition.

      IsBool(false) --> true *)

(** missing
    The absence of a value: a function that ends with no value gives it, as does Get on a key that is absent. A field typed
    with ? has it as its default. JSON writes it null.

      Get("a", {}) --> missing
      IsMissing(missing) --> true *)

(** it
    The parameter of a short function: an argument that holds it is a function of it, so Map(it * 2, xs) is Map(x -> x * 2, xs).
    After then, it is the value on the left.

      Map(it * 2, [1, 2]) --> [2, 4]
      Filter(it > 1, [1, 2, 3]) --> [2, 3] *)

(** as
    After a use, renames what the file sees: use std/queue as line gives line.Of.

      line.Count(line.Of([1, 2])) --> 2 *)

(** _
    In a pattern, takes anything and names nothing. A name has no _: words join by capitals, byteLength or ByteLength.

      match 9 1 -> "one" _ -> "any" end --> "any" *)

(** ..
    The integers from the one on its left to the one on its right, both included: a..b is Range(a, b). In the brackets of a
    list, it takes a part of it, and end there is the last position.

      1..4 --> [1, 2, 3, 4]
      Range(1, 4) --> [1, 2, 3, 4]
      tens = [10, 20, 30, 40]
      tens[2..3] --> [20, 30]
      tens[2..end] --> Skip(1, tens) *)

(** ?
    After a result, the value of an Ok, or else a way out of the function with the Err. After a type, the type allows missing:
    Text?.

      defn doubled(r) Ok(r? * 2) end
      doubled(Ok(4)) --> Ok(8)
      doubled(Err("no")) --> Err("no") *)
